{{ill|Shikaumi Shrine|ja|志賀海神社}} is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Fukuoka]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Wakabayashi |first=Haruko |title=The Mongol Invasions and the Making of the Iconography of Foreign Enemies: The Case of Shikaumi jinja engi |url=https://www.academia.edu/18701881/The_Mongol_Invasions_and_the_Making_of_the_Iconography_of_Foreign_Enemies_The_Case_of_Shikaumi_jinja_engi}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi Shrine |url=https://yokanavi.com/en/spot/26911/ |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=The Official Guide to Fukuoka City - yokanavi.com |language=en}}</ref> It is located on a [[Shika Island|Shikanoshima island]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shikanoshima Island |url=http://www.fukuoka-kotsu.co.jp/english/ |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=Fukuoka Kotsu |language=ja}}</ref> previously believed to have been occupied by the [[Azumi people]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi-jinja Shrine Okitsu-gu Shrine |url=https://visit-fukuoka-japan.com/todo/other_spots/400000009636/ |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=VISIT FUKUOKA |language=en}}</ref>

The famous [[King of Na gold seal]] was found on the same island.<ref>{{Cite book |title=National Treasures of Japan – Exhibition catalogue, April 10 - May 27, 1990, Tokyo National Museum |publisher=[[Yomiuri Shimbun]] |year=1990 |language=Japanese}}</ref>

It is linked to foreign war due to it being where [[Empress Jingū]] launched her invasion of Korea from, and it being a site of conflict during the [[Mongol invasions of Japan]].<ref name=":0" />

