| religious_affiliation = Shinto
| image                 = Shikaumi-jinja haiden.JPG
| deity                 = [[Watatsumi]]
}}{{Interlanguage link|Shikaumi Shrine|ja|志賀海神社}} is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Fukuoka]].<ref name=":02">{{Cite journal |last=Wakabayashi |first=Haruko |title=The Mongol Invasions and the Making of the Iconography of Foreign Enemies: The Case of Shikaumi jinja engi |url=https://www.academia.edu/18701881/The_Mongol_Invasions_and_the_Making_of_the_Iconography_of_Foreign_Enemies_The_Case_of_Shikaumi_jinja_engi}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi Shrine |url=https://yokanavi.com/en/spot/26911/ |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=The Official Guide to Fukuoka City - yokanavi.com |language=en}}</ref> It is located on [[Shika Island|Shikanoshima island]]<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Shikanoshima Island |url=http://www.fukuoka-kotsu.co.jp/english/ |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=Fukuoka Kotsu |language=ja}}</ref> where the [[Azumi people]] used to live.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi-jinja Shrine Okitsu-gu Shrine |url=https://visit-fukuoka-japan.com/todo/other_spots/400000009636/ |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=VISIT FUKUOKA |language=en}}</ref>

The famous [[King of Na gold seal]] was discovered on the same island as the Kumano Taisha shrine.<ref>{{Cite book |title=National Treasures of Japan – Exhibition catalogue, April 10 - May 27, 1990, Tokyo National Museum |publisher=[[Yomiuri Shimbun]] |year=1990 |language=Japanese}}</ref>

The shrine has historical connections to foreign wars because it was the place from which [[Empress Jingū]] launched her invasion of Korea, and it was also a site of conflict during the [[Mongol invasions of Japan]].<ref name=":02" />

The [[Azumi people]] traditionally followed the sea God, [[Watatsumi]].<ref name=":022">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref> The Shrine is considered their ancestral shrine and honors their god [[Watatsumi]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi Jinja Gojinkosai |url=http://www.dydo-matsuri.com/english/list/2015/shikaumi/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200131135054/http://www.dydo-matsuri.com/english/list/2015/shikaumi/ |archive-date=2020-01-31 |access-date=2020-04-16 |website=DyDo GROUP Matsuri of Japan}}</ref>

The shrine is taken care of by a priest who is believed to be a descendant of the Azumi clan. The biennial gojinkosai ceremony held at the shrine is recognized as an intangible cultural property by the prefecture.<ref name=":3" />

The shrine, incorporates a [[deer]] motif in its rituals and designs. The deer motif is significant to the Azumi people and their beliefs, and is a common feature in the historic culture of the region.<ref name=":02" />

