{{Interlanguage link|Shikaumi Shrine|ja|志賀海神社|simple}} is a [[Shinto shrine]] located in [[Fukuoka|Fukuoka, Kyushu, Japan]].<ref name=":02">{{Cite journal |last=Wakabayashi |first=Haruko |title=The Mongol Invasions and the Making of the Iconography of Foreign Enemies: The Case of Shikaumi jinja engi |url=https://www.academia.edu/18701881}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi Shrine |url=https://yokanavi.com/en/spot/26911/ |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=The Official Guide to Fukuoka City - yokanavi.com |language=en}}</ref> It is located on [[Shika Island|Shikanoshima island]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi-jinja Shrine Okitsu-gu Shrine |url=https://visit-fukuoka-japan.com/todo/other_spots/400000009636/ |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=VISIT FUKUOKA |language=en}}</ref>

The shrine has historical connections to foreign wars as it was the place from which [[Empress Jingū]] launched her invasion of Korea, and it was also a site of conflict during the [[Mongol invasions of Japan]].<ref name=":02" />

The [[Azumi people]], who inhabited [[Kyushu]], traditionally followed the sea deity, [[Watatsumi]].<ref name=":022">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref> Shikaumi Shrine is considered this deity's ancestral shrine, and also honors [[Watatsumi]]<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=Shikaumi Jinja Gojinkosai |url=http://www.dydo-matsuri.com/english/list/2015/shikaumi/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200131135054/http://www.dydo-matsuri.com/english/list/2015/shikaumi/ |archive-date=2020-01-31 |access-date=2020-04-16 |website=DyDo GROUP Matsuri of Japan}}</ref> and [[Azumi-no-isora]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=a0QKAQAAMAAJ&q=Azumi-no-isora |title=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |publisher=International Institute for the Study of Religions |year=1993}}</ref>

The primary revered figures of the shrine include, on the left, Nakatsu Watatsumi no Kami (middle depth god) and Empress Jingū; in the middle, Sokotsu Watatsumi no Kami (depths god) and Tamayorihime no Kami; and on the right, Uwatsu Watatsumi no Kami (sea surface god) and Emperor Ōjin.<ref name=":023">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=}}</ref>{{rp|xxvi}}

The three watatsumis are speculated to correspond to historical fishing practices<ref name=":023" />{{rp|xxviii}}

[[Empress Jingū]] and [[Emperor Ojin]] are both worshipped at the shrine due to their relationship with the myth of the invasion of the [[Three Han]].<ref name=":023" />{{rp|xxvii}} Empress Jingu had sex with the god [[Azumi-no-isora]] while pregnant with [[Emperor Ojin]] after he said from the womb that it was acceptable, and then Azumi no Isora gave her the [[tide jewels]].<ref name=":023" />{{rp|xxvii}} {{Interlanguage link|Watatsumi Shrine|ja|海神社 (神戸市)|simple|link=|prefix=|foreign=}} which was founded by [[Empress Jingu]] when she returned from the Three Han (三韓, Korea) campaign.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Shinto shrines {{!}} Japan {{!}} Rods Shinto |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/ |access-date=2023-04-04 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref> and also worships [[Watatsumi]]<ref name=":1w">{{Cite web |title=Watatsumi Shrine {{!}} 海神社 {{!}}Hyogo-ken, Kobe-shi |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/28-108-01 |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref> and [[Ikasuri Shrine]] and [[Ikuta Shrine]] which were also founded by her at the time.<ref name=":1w" />

The biennial gojinkosai ceremony held at the shrine is recognized as an intangible cultural property by the prefecture.<ref name=":3" />

The shrine incorporates a [[deer]] motif in its rituals and designs. The deer motif is significant to the Azumi people and their beliefs<ref name=":02" /><ref name=":024" />, and is a common feature in the historic culture of the region.<ref name=":02" /><ref name=":024">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref>

