[[:en:Empress Jingū|Empress Jingū]] and [[:en:Emperor Ojin|Emperor Ojin]] are both worshipped at the shrine due to their relationship with the myth of the invasion of the [[:en:Three Han|Three Han]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{rp|xxvii}} Empress Jingu had sex with the god [[Azumi-no-isora]] while pregnant with [[:en:Emperor Ojin|Emperor Ojin]] after he said from the womb that it was acceptable, and then Azumi no Isora gave her the [[:en:Tide jewels|Tide jewels]].<ref name="rambelli-2018a" />{{rp|xxvii}} [[Watatsumi Shrine (Kobe)|Watatsumi Shrine]] which was founded by [[:en:Empress Jingū|Empress Jingū]] when she returned from the Three Han (三韓, Korea) campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shinto shrines {{!}} Japan {{!}} Rods Shinto |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/ |access-date=2023-04-04 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref> and also worships [[:en:Watatsumi|Watatsumi]]<ref name=":1w">{{Cite web |title=Watatsumi Shrine {{!}} 海神社 {{!}}Hyōgo-ken, Kobe-shi |url=https://www.rodsshinto.com/28-108-01 |access-date=2023-04-06 |website=shintoshrines |language=en}}</ref> and [[Ikasuri Shrine]] and [[Ikuta Shrine]] which were also founded by her at the time.<ref name=":1w" />

The biennial gojinkosai ceremony held at the shrine is recognized as an intangible cultural property by the prefecture.<ref name="dydo-group-matsuri-of-japan-2020a" />

The shrine incorporates a [[:en:Deer|Deer]] motif in its rituals and designs. The deer motif is significant to the Azumi people and their beliefs,<ref name="wakabayashi-2" /><ref name="rambelli-2018b" /> and is a common feature in the historic culture of the region.<ref name="wakabayashi-2" /><ref name="rambelli-2018b">{{Cite book |last=Rambelli |first=F |title=The Sea and The Sacred in Japan |publisher=Bloomsbury Academic Publishing |year=2018 |isbn=978-1350062870 |location=Camden |pages=preface}}</ref>

